\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The Clifford algebra of the configuration form}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring, #{M} a #{k}-module and
#{q:M\to k} a \vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004}.
Use the [tensor algebra](ca-001H) and the square-quotient
convention for the \vocabk{Clifford algebra}{ca-0006}:
##{\operatorname{Cl}(q)=T_k(M)/I_q,}
where #{I_q} is the two-sided ideal generated by
#{v\otimes v-q(v)1} for all #{v\in M}.
The canonical linear map #{\iota_q:M\to\operatorname{Cl}(q)}
satisfies #{\iota_q(v)^2=q(v)1}. Scalars in this formula mean
their images under the algebra's structure map. No invertibility
of #{2} is required for this construction.}

\p{For every unital associative #{k}-algebra #{A}, a linear map
#{f:M\to A} extends to a unique unital #{k}-algebra homomorphism
#{\widehat f:\operatorname{Cl}(q)\to A} if and only if
##{f(v)^2=q(v)1_A\qquad(v\in M).}
This is the [Clifford lift](ca-000B) and its
[universal property](ca-000C).}

\proof{
\p{Necessity follows by applying an algebra homomorphism to the
square relation. For sufficiency, send a pure tensor to its ordered
product,
##{v_1\otimes\cdots\otimes v_r\longmapsto
f(v_1)\cdots f(v_r),}
and send #{c\in k=T_k^0(M)} to #{c1_A}.
The product is multilinear because #{f} is linear, multiplication
in #{A} is distributive, and the scalar images are central. The
tensor-product universal property therefore gives a linear map on
each tensor power. Their direct sum defines a linear map #{F:T_k(M)\to A}.
Concatenation of pure tensors becomes multiplication of their
ordered products. Distributivity then proves multiplicativity on
all finite sums, and the degree-zero formula preserves the unit
and scalars. Every generator of #{I_q} maps to
#{f(v)^2-q(v)1_A=0}; multiplicativity makes every element of the
two-sided ideal map to zero. Thus #{F} descends to #{\widehat f}.
Every tensor is a finite linear combination of pure tensors, whose
images in the quotient are products of the #{\iota_q(v)}.
An algebra map with the prescribed generator values must have the
displayed value on each such product and on every scalar. This
proves uniqueness.}
}

\p{Linearity and the square relation give the anticommutator:
##{\begin{gathered}
\iota_q(u)\iota_q(v)+\iota_q(v)\iota_q(u)\\
=\bigl(q(u+v)-q(u)-q(v)\bigr)1\\
=B_q(u,v)1.
\end{gathered}}
Indeed, expand #{(\iota_q(u)+\iota_q(v))^2} and subtract
the two squares. This uses no division by #{2}. When #{2} is
invertible, #{b_q=B_q/2} exists and the relation becomes
#{\iota_q(u)\iota_q(v)+\iota_q(v)\iota_q(u)=2b_q(u,v)1}.
Conversely that relation with #{u=v} recovers the square relation
by cancellation of #{2}. Thus the two presentations agree in
this case. Without that hypothesis, the anticommutator alone
need not recover the squares.}

\p{Meinrenken gives the bilinear presentation in
\citet{Definition 2.1, p. 27}{meinrenken2013clifford}
and the universal property in
\citet{Proposition 2.2 and Remark 2.2, p. 29}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
His chapter assumes a finite-dimensional vector space over a
characteristic-zero field (p. 23); Remark 2.2 includes the ordinary
algebra formulation. The direct argument above supplies the stated
commutative-ring generality. Mathlib's
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.ι_sq_scalar},
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.lift},
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.lift_unique} and
\leanref{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_add_swap}
provide these respective generic square, extension, uniqueness and
unhalved-polar statements.}

\p{Now take the real [configuration form](fdip-000O) and put
#{c_i=\iota_q(\delta_i)}. Write #{\{x,y\}=xy+yx} for the
anticommutator. The matrix entries give
##{\begin{aligned}
c_i^2&=s1\quad(i=0,1,2,3),\\
\{c_0,c_1\}&=\{c_2,c_3\}=-2\,1,\\
\{c_1,c_2\}&=\{c_3,c_0\}=-2t\,1,\\
\{c_0,c_2\}&=\{c_1,c_3\}=0.
\end{aligned}}
These follow from #{q(\delta_i)=Q_{ii}=s} and
#{b_q(\delta_i,\delta_j)=Q_{ij}}. In particular the squares
are positive in this convention. The
[opposite-sign convention](fcap-0008) would use #{-q}.}

\p{For this real form, both the scalar map and #{\iota_q} are
injective. Here is the \vocabk{exterior algebra}{ca-000E} argument of
\citet{Proposition 2.1, p. 28}{meinrenken2013clifford}, with its
operator identities made explicit. On #{\bigwedge V}, let
#{\epsilon(v)\omega=v\wedge\omega}, and define contraction by
##{d_v(1)=0,}
##{\begin{gathered}
d_v(w_1\wedge\cdots\wedge w_r)\\
=\sum_{j=1}^r(-1)^{j-1}b_q(v,w_j)\\
\quad\cdot w_1\wedge\cdots\widehat{w_j}\cdots\wedge w_r.
\end{gathered}}
The alternating multilinear expression defines a linear operator
on the exterior algebra. Exterior alternation gives
#{\epsilon(v)^2=0}. In #{d_v^2}, each pair of removed positions
occurs twice with opposite signs and the same scalar coefficient,
so #{d_v^2=0}. Separating the first term of #{d_v(v\wedge\omega)}
gives
##{d_v\epsilon(v)+\epsilon(v)d_v=b_q(v,v)I.}
Consequently #{C(v)=\epsilon(v)+d_v} is linear in #{v} and
#{C(v)^2=q(v)I}. The universal property gives a unital algebra
map #{\rho:\operatorname{Cl}(q)\to\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(\bigwedge V)}.
It satisfies #{\rho(\iota_q(v))1=v} and #{\rho(r1)1=r1}.
Since #{\bigwedge^1 V=V} and #{\bigwedge^0V=\mathbb R},
these equations prove both injections. The contraction identities
are also recorded in \citet{Section 2.1.3, pp. 25-26}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_injective} and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.algebraMap_injective}
give the corresponding injections for an arbitrary quadratic form
over a commutative ring with invertible #{2}.}

\p{The exterior action just used proves faithfulness on vectors
and scalars; it does not assert that #{\rho} is a faithful algebra
representation. Nor does this construction select a physical
spinor module, a positive expectation on the Clifford algebra, or
a differential operator. The earlier [Gaussian expectation](fdip-0006)
acts on polynomial observables of vertex fields; an expectation
on this new algebra would require a specified construction.}
